\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Chapter 3: Consumer Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
3.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consumer Theory I: Preferences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.2.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Alternatives space
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We study the behavior of a single agent when alternatives are consumption
 bundles of 
\begin_inset Formula $L\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

 commodities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The set of alternatives, or 
\emph on
consumption set
\emph default
, is 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, whose elements are the consumption bundles that the individual can conceivably
 consume given the physical constraints imposed by his environment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The generic consumption bundle is a vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x\equiv(x_{1},\ldots,x_{L})\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

; for each 
\begin_inset Formula $l=1,\ldots,L$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{l}$
\end_inset

 is the amount of commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 consumed at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Sometimes it will be useful to write a consumption vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 in matrix notation as 
\begin_inset Formula $x\equiv(x_{1},\ldots,x_{L})^{T}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For simplicity, we will usually assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 One special feature of the set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 is that it is 
\emph on
convex
\emph default
.
 That is, if two consumption bundles 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 are both elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, then the bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime\prime}=\alpha x+(1-\alpha)x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is also an element of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 for any 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We will study: (1) consumer preferences; (2) utility representation of consumer
 preferences; (3) consumer choice; and (4) revealed preference theory in
 the consumer's world.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.2.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consumer preferences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, we study the representation of a consumer by a preference relation
 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(41).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a binary relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
upper contour set of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 
\series default
is 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succsim,x)\equiv\{y\in X:y\succsim x)$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
indifference set of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 at x
\series default
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $I(\succsim,x)\equiv\{y\in X:y\sim x\}$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
lower contour set of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset


\series default
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\succsim,x)\equiv\{y\in X:x\succsim y\}$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
strict upper and lower contour sets of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset


\series default
 are 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succ,x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\succ,x)$
\end_inset

, respectively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Desirability assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is often reasonable to assume that larger amounts of commodities are
 preferred to smaller ones.
 This feautre of preferences is caputred in the assumption of monotonicity.
 For Definition 43, we assume that the consumption of larger amounts of
 goods is always feasible in principle; that is, if 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\geq x$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(42).

\series default
 
\emph on
We use
\emph default
 
\emph on
the following notation for vector inequalities: for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\geq y$
\end_inset

 if for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,L$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\geq y_{i}$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $x\gneq y$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $x\geq y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x\neq y$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $x\gg y$
\end_inset

 if for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,L$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}>y_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The following are further restrictions that are usually assumed on consumer
 preferences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(43).
 
\series default
\emph on
A binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 is:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Strictly Monotone 
\series default
iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:x\gneq y\implies x\succ y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Monotone 
\series default
iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:x\gg y\implies x\succ y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Weakly Monotone 
\series default
iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:x\geq y\implies x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Locally Non-Satiated 
\series default
iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in X,\ \forall\ \epsilon\in\mathbb{R}_{++},\ \exists\ y\in B_{\epsilon}^{X}(x):y\succ x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Remark*
Monotonicity alone does not imply weak monotonicity, and weak monotonicity
 does not imply local non-satiation.
 For instance, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 defined by: 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subset X:x\sim y$
\end_inset

.
 Clearly, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is weakly monotone, but it is not locally non-satiated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The assumption that preferences are monotone is satisfies as long as commodities
 are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

good
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 rather than 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bads.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Even if some commodity is a bad, however, we may still be able to view
 preferences as monotone because it is often possible to redefine a consumption
 activity in a way that satisfies the assumption.
 For example, if one commdotiy is garbage, we can instead define the individual'
s consumption over the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

absence of garbage.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that if 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is monotone, we may have indfference with respect to an increase in the
 amount of some but not all commodities.
 In contrast, strong monotonicity says that if 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is larger than 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 for 
\emph on
some
\emph default
 commodity and is no less for any other, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is strictly prefered to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Local nonsatiation is a weaker desirability assumption than monotonicity.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, local nonsatiation rules out the extreme situation in which all commodities
 are bads, since in that case no consumption at all (the point 
\begin_inset Formula $x=0$
\end_inset

) would be a satiation point.
 One implicaiton of local nonsatiation (and, hence, of monotonicity) is
 that it rules out 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

thick
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 indifference sets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a binary relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is transitive, weakly monotone, and locally non-satiated, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is monotone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 be such that 
\begin_inset Formula $y\gg x$
\end_inset

.
 We prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ x$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose by means of contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg(y\succ x)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y\gg x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is weakly monotone, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg(y\succ x)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\sim y$
\end_inset

.
 We claim that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $z\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\leq z\leq y$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $z\sim x$
\end_inset

.
 To prove this, observe that since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is weakly monotone, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succsim z\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

, then by transitivity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\sim z$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $z^{*}\equiv\frac{x+y}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y\gg x$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y\gg z^{*}\gg x$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\equiv\min_{i=1,\ldots,L}\frac{\left|y_{i}-x_{i}\right|}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\epsilon}^{X}(z^{*})\subseteq\left\{ z\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:x\leq z\leq y\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 To prove this, let 
\begin_inset Formula $z\in B_{\epsilon}^{X}(z^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\leq z$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose by means of contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\nleq z$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, there is 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in\left\{ 1,\ldots,L\right\} $
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{k}<x_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{k}^{*}>x_{k}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|z_{k}^{*}-z_{k}\right|\geq\left|z_{k}^{*}-x_{k}\right|\geq\epsilon$
\end_inset

.
 Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert z^{*}-z\right\Vert =\left(\sum_{l=1}^{L}(z_{l}^{*}-z_{l})^{2}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}\geq\left|z_{k}^{*}-z_{k}\right|$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert z^{*}-z\right\Vert \geq\epsilon$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z\notin B_{\epsilon}^{X}(z^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 However, this is a contradiction.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\leq z$
\end_inset

.
 A symmetric argument shows that 
\begin_inset Formula $z\leq y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We conclude that there is 
\begin_inset Formula $z^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

 such that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $z\in B_{\epsilon}^{X}(z^{*})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\leq z\leq y$
\end_inset

 and consequently, 
\begin_inset Formula $z\sim x\sim z^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, there is no 
\begin_inset Formula $z\in B_{\epsilon}^{X}(z^{*})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $z\succ z^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts local non-satiation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Convexity Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A second significant assumption, that of convexity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, concerns the trade-offs that the consumer is willing to make among different
 goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(44).
 
\series default
\emph on
A set 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
convex
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq Y,\ \forall\ \alpha\in[0,1]:\alpha x+(1-\alpha)y\in Y$
\end_inset

.
 Here 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha x$
\end_inset

 is the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha x_{1},\ldots,\alpha x_{L})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is a convex set.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(45).

\series default
 
\emph on
A binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
convex
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in X:U(\succsim,x)\ \mbox{is convex}$
\end_inset

; that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{y,z\}\subseteq U(\succsim,x),\ \forall\ \alpha\in[0,1]:\alpha y+(1-\alpha)z\in U(\succsim,x)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\series default
Convexity is a strong but central hypothesis in economics.
 It can be interpreted in terms of 
\emph on
diminishing marginal rates of substitution
\emph default
: That is, with convex preferences, from any initial consumption situation
 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, and for any tow commodities, it takes increasily larger amounts of one
 commodity to compensate for successive unit losses of the other.
 More generally, convexity is equivalent to a diminishing marginal rate
 of substitution between any two goods, provided that we allow for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

composite commodities
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 formed from linear combinations of the 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 basic commodities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Convexity can also be viewed as the formal expression of a basic inclination
 of economic agents for diversification.
 A taste of diversificaiton is a realistic trait of economic life.
 Economic theory would be in serious difficulty if this postulated propensity
 for diversificaiton did not have significant descriptive content.
 But there is no doubt that one can easily think of choice situations where
 it is violated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We also make use at times of a strengthening of the convexity assumption.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(46).

\series default
 
\emph on
A binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
strictly convex
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in X,\ \forall\ \{y,z\}\subseteq U(\succsim,x),\ \forall\ \alpha\in(0,1):y\neq z\implies\alpha y+(1-\alpha)z\succ x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Continuity Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\series default
In the next section, it will be shown that continuous preferences is the
 assumption needed to ensure the existence of a utility function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(47).

\series default
 
\emph on
A binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
continuous
\series default
 iff it is a closed subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $X\times X$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\forall\; x^{t}\underset{t\to\infty}{\longrightarrow}x,\ \forall\ y^{t}\underset{t\to\infty}{\longrightarrow}y:(\forall\ t\in\mathbb{N}:x^{t}\succsim y^{t})\implies x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Continuity says that the consumer's preferences are preserved under limits,
 i.e., it cannot exhibit 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

jumps.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 For example, it prevents the consumer preferring each element in sequence
 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x^{t}\}$
\end_inset

 to the corresponding element in sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\{y^{t}\}$
\end_inset

 but suddenly reversing her preference at the limiting ponts of these sequences
 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(48).

\series default
 
\emph on
A binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
continuous*
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in X$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succsim,x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\succsim,x)$
\end_inset

 are closed in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Continuity* says that for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, the upper and lower contour sets include their boundaries.
 If the binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is a preference, then continuity* is an equivalent way to state the notion
 of continuity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
In general, continuity implies continuity*, but the reverse implication
 may not hold.
 However, from Theorem 7, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is complete and transitive, then continuity is equivalent to continuity*.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\emph on
(7).
 
\series default
\emph default
A preference relation is continuous if and only if it is continuous*.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
We prove both directions independently.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous*, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in X$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succsim,x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\succsim,x)$
\end_inset

 are closed in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{(y^{t})\}_{t=1}^{\infty}\subseteq U(\succsim,x)$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{t}\rightarrow y$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ t\in\mathbb{N}:y^{t}\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 Also, note that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\rightarrow x$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in U(\succsim,x)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succsim,x)$
\end_inset

 is a closed set in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 By the same arguement, 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\succsim,x)$
\end_inset

 is also a closed set in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous*.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Leftarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous*.
 We prove that it is continuous, i.e., for any two convergent sequences 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}\rightarrow x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{t}\rightarrow y$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ t\in\mathbb{N}:x^{t}\succsim y^{t}$
\end_inset

, we have that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose by means of contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is not continuous, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\; x^{t}\underset{t\to\infty}{\longrightarrow}x,\ \exists\ y^{t}\underset{t\to\infty}{\longrightarrow}y:(\forall\ t\in\mathbb{N}:x^{t}\succsim y^{t})\ \wedge\ y\succ x$
\end_inset

 Then, we claim that there is an open set containing 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $V(y)$
\end_inset

, and an open set containing 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $V(x)$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ y^{\prime}\in V(y),\forall\ x^{\prime}\in V(x):y^{\prime}\succ x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 There are two cases.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ z\in X$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ z\succ x$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $V(y)\equiv U(\succ,z)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $V(x)\equiv L(\succ,z)$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ z\implies y\in V(y)$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $z\succ x\implies x\in V(x)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous*, 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\succsim,z)$
\end_inset

 and 
