Parametric and simplicity assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In applied work, it is useful to restric attention to families of preferences
 that can be generated by one of its indifference sets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Homothetic
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(49).

\series default
 
\emph on
A preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is homothetic iff its indifference sets are related by proportional expansion
 along rays, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq X,\ \forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}:x\sim y\implies\alpha x\sim\alpha y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Quasilinear
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(50).

\series default
 
\emph on
The preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
quasilinear with respect to commodity 1
\series default
 iff:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq X,\ \forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}:(x_{1},\ldots,x_{L})\sim(y_{1},\ldots,y_{L})\implies(x_{1}+\alpha,\ldots,x_{L})\sim(y_{1}+\alpha,\ldots,y_{L})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ (x_{1},\ldots,x_{L})\in X,\ \forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{++}:(x_{1}+\alpha,\ldots,x_{L})\succ(x_{1},\ldots,x_{L})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Definition*

\emph on
A preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
quasilinear with respect to commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset


\series default
 iff the above conditions hold with respect to commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Cobb-Douglas
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(51).

\series default
 
\emph on
A preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\series bold
Cobb-Douglas
\series default
 preference iff it is represented by a utility funciton 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 of the form, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\equiv(x_{1},x_{2})\in X\mapsto u(x)\equiv x_{1}^{\alpha}x_{2}^{1-\alpha}$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
CES
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(52).

\series default
 
\emph on
A preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\series bold
CES
\series default
 (Constant Elasticity of Substitution) preference iff it is represented
 by a utility funciton 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $x\equiv(x_{1},x_{2})\in X\mapsto u(x)\equiv(\alpha_{1}x_{1}^{\rho}+\alpha_{2}x_{2}^{\rho})^{\frac{1}{\rho}}$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \alpha_{1},\alpha_{2}\right\} \in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

 and 
