\begin_layout Problem
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Prove that: (a) Strict monotonicity implies monotonicity; (b) Monotonicity
 implies local non-satiation; (c) The converse statements are not true.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
(a) Strict monotonicity implies monotonicity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let the binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be strictly monotone.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\gg y$
\end_inset

.
 Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\gg y\implies x\gneq y$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is strictly monotone, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x\gneq y\implies x\succ y$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\gg y$
\end_inset

, we have that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succ y$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is monotone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
(b) Monotonicity implies local non-satiation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let the binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be monotone.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and take 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

.
 Now, define 
\begin_inset Formula $y\equiv\left(y_{i}=x_{i}+\frac{\epsilon}{2}\right),\ i=i,\ldots,L$
\end_inset

.
 Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in B_{\epsilon}^{X}(x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\gg x$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is monotone, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ x$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \epsilon\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ y\in B_{\epsilon}^{X}(x):y\succ x$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is locally non-satiated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
(c) The converse statements are not true.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Leontief preferences are monotone, but they are not strictly monotone.
 Now, consider the preference where points with smaller Euclidean distance
 from zero are preferred to points with larger Euclidan distance from zero,
 but zero is the least preferred point in 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 This preference is locally non-satiated, but it is not monotone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Recall that a preference 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 is convex if for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succsim,x)$
\end_inset

 is convex.
 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is rational.
 Prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is convex if and only if for each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in[0,1],\alpha x+(1-\alpha)y\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is rational.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is convex.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L},\forall\ \alpha\in[0,1]:x\succsim y\implies\alpha x+(1-\alpha)y\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

, which implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in U(\succsim,y)$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is complete, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succsim y$
\end_inset

, which implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in U(\succsim,y)$
\end_inset

.
 By definition of convexity, 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succsim,y)$
\end_inset

 is convex.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq U(\succsim,y)\ \wedge\ \alpha\in[0,1]\implies\alpha x+(1-\alpha)y\in U(\succsim,y)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha x+(1-\alpha)y\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Leftarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L},\ \forall\ \alpha\in[0,1]:x\succsim y\implies\alpha x+(1-\alpha)y\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is convex, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ y\in X$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succsim,y)$
\end_inset

 is convex.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in X$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succsim,y)$
\end_inset

 is convex if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,z\}\subseteq U(\succsim,y),\forall\ \alpha\in[0,1]:\alpha x+(1-\alpha)z\in U(\succsim,y)$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,z\}\subseteq U(\succsim,y)$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
