\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The proof can be found in Section 3.2.2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Construct a quasilinear preference that is not continuous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Define 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{lex}$
\end_inset

 as follows: for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\equiv(x_{1},x_{2})\in\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $y\equiv(y_{1},y_{2})\in\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{+},\ x\succsim_{lex}y\iff\left[\left(x_{1}>y_{1}\right)\ \vee\ \left(x_{1}=y_{1}\ \wedge\ x_{2}\geq y_{2}\right)\right]$
\end_inset

.
 Important to note, with this preference relation, indifference sets are
 just singletons.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}:I(\succsim,x)\equiv\{y\in X:y\sim x\}=\{x\}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we will show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{lex}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 is quasilinear with respect to commodity 1.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

.
 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x\sim y$
\end_inset

.
 From the discussion in the previous paragraph, 
\begin_inset Formula $x=y$
\end_inset

 must hold.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{1}+\alpha,x_{2})\sim(y_{1}+\alpha,y_{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Next, take 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

.
 By the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{lex}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{1}+\alpha,x_{2})\succ(x_{1},x_{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{lex}$
\end_inset

 is quasilinear with respect to commodity 1.
 However, by Problem 1, Section 3.3.3, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{lex}$
\end_inset

 is not a continuous preference.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can also construct a binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 by representing it with a utility function.
 By Proposition 1 in the class notes, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 will be complete and transitive.
 Define the utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u(x_{1},x_{2}) & = & x_{1}+g(x_{2})\\
g(x_{2}) & = & \begin{cases}
x_{2} & \mbox{if}\ x_{2}<\frac{1}{4}\\
10+x_{2} & \mbox{if}\ \frac{1}{4}\leq x_{2}<\frac{1}{2}\\
11-x_{2} & \mbox{if}\ \frac{1}{2}\leq x_{2}<\frac{3}{4}\\
1-x_{2} & \mbox{if}\ \frac{3}{4}\leq x_{2}<1
\end{cases}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The preference represented by this utility function is quasilinear with
 respect to commodity 1, but it is not continuous.
\end_layout

